11.2  Public-key cryptography

TL;DR
  - A key pair: the private key stays secret, the public one can go anywhere.
    What one does, only the other can check or undo.
  - Signatures: sign with the private key, anyone verifies with the public one.
    That's how SSH logins, package updates and certificates work.
  - Key exchange: two strangers agree on a shared secret over an open network,
    then switch to fast symmetric encryption.

      ssh-keygen -t ed25519            # a key pair for SSH
      gpg --verify file.sig file       # check a signed download

On your machine
  Keys      id_ed25519 (ssh-ed25519), id_rsa_old (ssh-rsa) open to others (644):
            ssh refuses it
  Config    3 host entries in ~/.ssh/config, with jump hosts
  Known     4 host keys trusted in known_hosts, names hashed
  Login     1 key may log in here as you (authorized_keys)
  Agent     none in this session: keys with a passphrase ask for it every time
  Server    port 2222 · password login no · root login prohibit-password
            (default)

Pitfalls
  - Whoever has your private key is you: protect it with a passphrase, and never
    copy it to servers.
  - RSA keys under 2048 bits are too weak today; Ed25519 is shorter, faster and
    safer to use.
  - A public key alone proves nothing about who owns it: you need a trusted way
    to get it (a certificate, a fingerprint checked in person).

Deep dive

  ▾ What makes it work?

    Problems easy one way and hard the other. RSA rests on factoring: easy to
    multiply two large primes, infeasible to find them from the product.
    Elliptic curves (Ed25519, X25519) rest on a similar one-way operation on
    curve points, with much smaller keys for the same strength.

  ▾ What do quantum computers change?

    A large enough quantum computer would break RSA and elliptic curves. Data
    recorded today could be decrypted later, so key exchanges are moving first:
    OpenSSH 10 and current browsers combine X25519 with ML-KEM, a post-quantum
    algorithm, so both would have to fall. Symmetric encryption and hashes only
    need longer keys.

Related
  Commands  ssh-keygen, gpg
  Paths     ~/.ssh/, ~/.gnupg/
  See also  idk 11.1 Hashes, encryption and signatures
  See also  idk 11.3 Certificates: how HTTPS proves who it is
  See also  idk 29.1 SSH keys and the agent
